# empty except (13) and local-only assignment (67) fire; reraise (22),
# on-E-do-Log (31), Result assignment (43), and out-param assignment (53)
# do not -- Task 9c: an assignment to Result or a var/out parameter converts
# the exception into a status the caller must inspect (the TryXxx idiom), so
# it counts as handling. An assignment to a plain local does not.
#
# 2026-08-13, OWNER RULING: a DOCUMENTED deliberate swallow is accepted -- an
# except body that runs no code and explains in writing why dropping the
# exception is the lesser evil (84). Case 13 was previously written as an
# "empty" handler that still carried `// swallowed -- nothing here`, which is
# exactly the newly-accepted shape; it is now genuinely empty, so it still
# tests what it was named for. Its line number is unchanged -- the comment was
# replaced by a blank line rather than deleted -- because every expectation
# below is anchored by line.
try-except-swallowed 13
!try-except-swallowed 22
!try-except-swallowed 31
!try-except-swallowed 43
!try-except-swallowed 53
try-except-swallowed 67
!try-except-swallowed 84
# v(2026-08-13b): a CALL carrying the exception's own .Message/.ClassName is
# reporting, whatever the sink is named (99) -- but handing that same text to a
# plain LOCAL is still a swallow (116). The pair is the point: 99 alone would
# also pass a naive "mentions .Message" check, and 116 is what proves the test
# is on the call and not on the substring.
!try-except-swallowed 99
try-except-swallowed 116
# Same observability test applied to a member access: writing the exception
# THROUGH an object is reporting (129), writing an unrelated value to a field is
# still a swallow (142). 142 is the guard -- accepting any exprDot LHS would
# pass 129 and gut the rule.
!try-except-swallowed 129
try-except-swallowed 142
